+/* ---- The 8x8 inverse transform ----
+ * FFmpeg's simple IDCT (simple_idct_template.c, 8 bits), the one the encoder's pictures are made with too, so
+ * every pixel must come out the same: in C it is some 350 integer multiplies a block, about 3,400 cycles on this CPU
+ * (decbench, 2026-11-25), and VMX128 has no integer multiply at all. It does multiply floats, and a float holds
+ * integers up to 2^24 exactly. So each 16-bit input is split into its low 8 bits and the rest, both small enough
+ * that their products with the transform's constants, and the sums of up to four of those, stay under 2^24:
+ * computed in floats they are exact whatever the rounding mode, and the two halves are put together as integers.
+ * The products with W4 = 2^14 - 1 are a shift and a subtraction. Sums of 32 bits wrap as the C code's do. */
